WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.780
Bringing money back to today.

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.040
Say you've won a scholarship that pays you

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.200
$2,000 a year for the next three years.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:09.020
That's $6,000 in all.

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:12.970
Now, someone offers to
swap it for $5,500 paid

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.400
right now today should you take the deal.

00:00:17.787 --> 00:00:19.587
To answer that, we need to know what

00:00:19.637 --> 00:00:21.437
money in the future is worth today.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:24.157
Let's start with money that grows.

00:00:25.047 --> 00:00:26.957
Say you can save at 6% a year.

00:00:27.007 --> 00:00:33.157
If you put in about $0.94 today, in
a year, it will have grown to $1.

00:00:33.527 --> 00:00:37.557
So $1 a year from now is
worth about $0.94 today,

00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:40.567
assuming you have access
to a savings account

00:00:40.617 --> 00:00:43.117
or something else paying you 6% a year.

00:00:43.987 --> 00:00:47.357
That works for any number of years.

00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:51.737
A dollar that arrives in two
years has to come back two steps,

00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:54.297
so it's worth about $0.89 today.

00:00:54.347 --> 00:00:56.977
A dollar in three years comes back

00:00:57.027 --> 00:00:59.657
three steps, and it's worth about $0.84.

00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:03.157
The longer the wait, the
less it's worth today.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.920
Now, let's stack those up.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:11.280
$0.94 plus $0.89 plus
$0.84 is about $2.67.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.920
That's what $1 a year for three
years is worth today at 6%.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.040
This number has a name. It's
called the annuity factor.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.610
Your scholarship pays
$2,000 a year, not $1.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:24.790
So multiply.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:32.270
$2,000 times the annuity
factor, 2.673, is $5,346.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:38.850
Three payments of $2,000
are worth $5,346 today.

00:01:39.213 --> 00:01:41.543
For three payments, stacking by hand is

00:01:41.593 --> 00:01:43.923
easy, but for 30 payments, it isn't.

00:01:44.393 --> 00:01:46.883
So there's a formula that gives
the height of the stack directly.

00:01:47.553 --> 00:01:51.643
For 30 payments at 6%, it gives 13.76.

00:01:51.723 --> 00:01:55.673
So should you take $5,500 today?

00:01:56.483 --> 00:02:00.413
Your scholarship is worth $5,346 today.

00:02:00.603 --> 00:02:04.433
So $5,500 is the better deal by $154.
